\f1\fs22 The hover limousine seemed to know exactly where to go, Tony surmised due to the redemption code he\'92d fed the booking app on his tablet. The code was on the only text in the next document to open on Halo\'92s thumb drive. Banking away from the hover pad, the limousine found an easy arc of lightly trafficked airspace corridor that took him directly downtown. Tony casually wondered about the kind of authority needed for such a direct flight while he rifled through bottles in the minibar. He sneered at the expensive brands of whiskies and brandies, bourbons and vodkas; all would be too refined for his cheap tastes. Nevertheless, Tony decided, wherever the journey took him, he need a decent dose of ingested courage. Hating Tasmanian scotch more than any other brown liquid, he thought he\'92d hate the expensive stuff less, and it might taste coarse enough that he might, almost enjoy it. He pulled the stopper out with his teeth, spat it away, and drank a large draft straight from the bottle. He hated it, but not so much to stop him taking a second draft and, as the limousine\'92s almost silent fans propelled him towards the hover pad stalk of what was obviously Hardy Tower, he swilled a third.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony\'92s usually dim curiosity was piqued when the limousine veered up from the hover pads over the  railway, public promenades and concourse outside the main entrance\'97which today, were only a floor or two above the roiling Layer\'97and circled around with majestic ease to a private pad that sprouted from the rear of the building. The hover pad was as black and dull as a rubber tyre, and huge\'97much larger than the limousine required. Almost entirely encircling it, a wide band of flowers beds bloomed in a variety of colours both bright and pastel which, if Tony had any knowledge of flowers, wold recognise as roses.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The limousine landed softly as if on a huge fluffy pillow, and opened it port passenger door. When Tony alighted, he took the scotch bottle.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He located a break in the flower beds and followed the black path between toward the building. Amid the roses (not that Tony recognised them as such) the path split in two and, although he had forgotten Halo\'92s silly verses, he took the right path. The garden was much larger than Tony might have guessed and, as he gazed about unappreciatively, and savoured none of the sense that he could be on the ground, not hundreds of metres in the air with a toxic cloud a few storeys below. Ahead, the path merged with a promenade that clung to the building and followed its curve out of sight to the left. Tony\'92s eyes were drawn up to where the tower seemed to press against the impossibly blue sky, so stark and blue that it appeared to be made of the richest of fabrics rather than thin air. Tony lost all sense of perspective as he gazed up. His chin jutted forward in such a way that an observer might believe it was pulling the rest of his body along with it.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>That\'92s when Tony tripped. He might not have fallen so hard had he not tried to save the whisky bottle. The expensive grog was saved, but Tony bit his lip in the fall. Tending a finger to seeping blood, he looked back for what had tripped him. A thick black irrigation hose had been ripped from the garden beds and ran along the side of the path for some metres before it snaked across to the other side and continued onwards out of sight. Curious, Tony cast his gaze farther on. A naked, old, greyish man by the blank wall of the tower, was posing as if taking a shower. Tony sharpened his gaze and saw the sparkle of water trickling over the man from what he surmised was the end of a repurposed irrigation hose taped to the wall. Beyond the shower scene, advertising hoarding leaned against the building to form the man\'92s makeshift home, a home Tony was sure would be dismantled by security guards before the day was out.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>While wringing out his clothes, the naked scrubber side-eyed Tony with suspicion as he passed by on the other side of a garden bed.\
