<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable> Although Tony blinked his eyes and shook his head, no clarity of thought came. Not that Tony expected clarity; he knew he wasn\'92t the brightest bulb in the amusement park. But while his mind fizzled its filament, his body reacted. He leaned in, pressing his nose ever closer to the rose on the wall. Or was it in front of the wall? Or in the wall? Tony sense of touch failed him; as before, he felt nothing, only emptiness, thin  and cool. Tony closed his eyes so he could focus on leaning forward slowly (so he wouldn\'92t break his nose). His face felt chill, and his eyebrows tingled. Other than that, nothing. He leaned in a little more, and the light shining on his closed eyelids brightened. Reflexively, his eyes bunted open. What Tony saw made him recoil quickly until he snapped to attention, bolt upright, his nose a few centimetres from the wall. What cognitive powers Tony possessed all snuffed out in that moment and he became a frightened animal obeying a primitive instinct to assess danger. So, furtively, he leaned forward again, this time willing his eyes open. He felt the chill and the tingling again, and saw darkness close around him as if he was pressing his face into a very feathery pillow. The pillow yielded easily, as if Tony\'92s face had passed entirely though. On the other side a wide space opened before him, an atrium filled with greenery like the lobby of an expensive hotel. Again, Tony snapped back, and was once more standing mere centimetres from a solid, opalescent, black wall.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He blinked and tried to process what he\'92d witnessed. He failed. So, in typical Tony style, when faced with an inexplicable circumstance, Tony resorted to his usual, final back-up plan: he did something stupid.\
