\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>It didn\'92t. And neither was his toe stubbed, his knee ruptured, his gonads crushed, nor his ribs cracked as he, quite literally, walked into a wall.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>And there it was again in front of him: a huge, open indoor space of opulence, of greenery and running water, and cool perfumed air. Behind him, a window with a view to the promenade and rose gardens from which he had stepped. As the shock of it subsided, fear took control of his musculoskeletal system and hurled him back through the window.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Are you gonna drink that?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony almost jumped out of his clothes with fright. Before him, the old, greyish man pointed to the whisky bottle in his hand.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Are you gonna wear some pants?\'92 Tony asked the still naked man.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Too late for that,\'92 the man said. \'91I need a drink. I got snakes in me boots.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You\'92re not wearing boots.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The man looked at Tony like he\'92d caught him cheating at cards. \'91I got the jim-jams, the rattles \'85 you know.\'92 When it was clear Tony didn\'92t now, he added, \'91I got the booze blues. I haven\'92t had a drink in two days.\'92 He licked his lips as he trained his milky eyes on the bottle.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony hesitated; perhaps the adrenaline going sour in his system slowed him down.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Look. I\'92m shaking all over,\'92 the old man said, and Tony winced as he involuntarily looked at nether parts where the man shook. \'91You gotta help me,\'92 the man pleaded. \'91I\'92m hallucinating.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Aren\'92t we all?\'92 Tony asked as he relented and handed over the bottle.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Even with shaky hands, the man swiftly 
\f2\i gonked
\f1\i0  the stopper out of the bottle and greedily guzzled three long nips from it. Wiping his mouth with the back of his surprisingly grimy hand, he said, \'91I could swear on my mother\'92s liquor licence I saw you stick your head in that wall.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You mean like this?\'92 Tony stuck his head in the wall. The man wailed and clutched the scotch to his bosom.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You try it,\'92 Tony instructed, after he\'92d pulled his head out.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What? Are you crazy?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Nah, look,\'92 Tony said, and demonstrated. \'91It\'92s easy.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The old man looked at Tony and the wall, and appeared to be considering it. \'91Nah,\'92 he said at last.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Oh, come on. Amuse me,\'92 Tony pleaded. \'91For the grog.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The old man clutched the bottle tighter to his chest, assessing whether Tony might try to seize it back. But all he saw was an innocent smile on Tony\'92s face, a look of wonder, a sense of adventure.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Oh all right.\'92 The old man, figuring he should match the speed and confidence of Tony\'92s demonstration, pushed his head towards the wall. Tony heard a loud crack as bone collided with stony wall. \'91Ow!\'92 the old man exclaimed as he put his fingers tenderly to his forehead, then examined his fingertips for blood. \'91You\'92ve given me a concussion, you idiot,\'92 he said then took a large swig of the whisky as medicine for the pain.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony tangled his eyebrows together, perplexed. \'91Why would it work only for me?\'92 And to test it out, poked his arm to his elbow into the wall.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91How would I know? Maybe it knows you, or something.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Knows me?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Yeah, like you own the place or something.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony laughed that off. \'91I\'92ve never owned much of anything in my life.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You and me both, brother,\'92 the old man said, his cloudy eyes becoming clearer as he drank another half a nip from the bottle.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony thought. \'91Try it again,\'92 he said, \'91while I\'92ve got my arm in there.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The old man looked sceptical, but slowly pushed his hand towards the wall. His eyes bulged with surprise as his fingers, then palm, then most of his forearm slid into the wall. \'91Yikes!\'92 he said and quickly pulled out.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91There\'92s a whole seven-star hotel in there,\'92 Tony said. \'91Wanna check it out with me?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The old man\'92s eyes turned greedy and he licked his lips again. \'91Hotel, you say?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91It sure looks like it,\'92 Tony said. \'91We might find you a decent set of clothes too.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Well, I ain\'92t proud,\'92 the man explained.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Yes, I can see that.\'92 Tony grabbed the man\'92s shoulder. \'91C\'92mon,\'92 he said, as he stepped into the wall and pulled the man with him. Into the cool, fresh air of the building\'92s interior he stepped, revelling in the restorative sensations of greenery and perfume, and was only dimly aware of the sound of breaking glass and a series of wet, slushing slaps behind him as suddenly the old man\'92s shoulder fell heavily away from his guiding hand.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony couldn\'92t make sense of the carnage behind him.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The moment Tony passed fully through, the wall re-solidified, slicing the man through his shoulder blades in a guillotine-straight diagonal to below his navel. Tony stared in frozen horror at the man\'92s startled face and watched an emulsion of spittle and whisky trickle from the corner of his dead, slack mouth.\

